WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
This is one synthetic week in a small volunteer group:
214 messages and only three closed decisions.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:17.000
Median decision time is 3.1 days. The problem is not
a lack of communication.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Commitments disappear inside the conversation.
This is demonstration data, not a pilot result.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Most agents optimize for how often they can help.
Quorum optimizes for how rarely it has to appear.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Every person gets a hard budget of two interruptions per rolling week.
When it is spent, Quorum must reroute, batch, wait, or use the safe default.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Now I will replay that same synthetic week. Quorum turns
scattered promises into a Commitment Ledger.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Every entry retains its source-message reference
and a verbatim evidence span.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:25.000
If the source cannot be proven, the ledger rejects the extraction
instead of inventing certainty.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Three low-risk actions can complete without another group discussion:
a tentative calendar event, a Gmail draft, and a form request.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:55.000
The draft is never sent. Each action produces one short group receipt.
The signed undo path expires after 24 hours and can be used only once.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:08.000
When judgment is required, Quorum does not poll the room.
It asks only the smallest sufficient set of accountable people.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Every question states its timeout and default.
Only two people are contacted in this replay.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Neither can exceed two requests in seven days.
If no safe quorum remains, the action is deferred.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:43.000
The main path is a five-node Strands Graph: Listener, Ledger Curator,
Risk Appraiser, Quorum Router, and Executor.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Risk and routing are deterministic. Model prose cannot change
the score, quorum, timeout, autonomy, or interruption spend.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Strands Swarm is used only for bounded semantic ambiguity.
A native hook interrupt guards every tool call and fails closed.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:20.000
The CodeZip reached READY in AgentCore Runtime. Its disabled model path
returned HTTP 503, then the workflow deleted the short-lived deployment.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Here is the same synthetic scenario side by side:
214 raw messages and three closed decisions.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Quorum appears six times, closes six decisions, and moves median
decision time from 74.4 hours to seven hours.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:56.000
The scenario reports a 16.7 percent undo rate,
so reversals stay visible instead of hidden.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:06.000
These numbers demonstrate the measurement contract.
They are not measured community impact.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:15.000
A 50-case synthetic gold set reports precision, miss rate,
and hallucination rate with strict source grounding.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:25.000
There is no participant quotation in this cut because no real organization
has provided consented data or approved wording.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:35.000
A real quote will replace this segment only after the participant
approves the exact sentence and its public use.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Quorum asks not how much an agent can say, but how much coordination
it can complete before it needs to speak.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
The public replay and source are available now.
The submission shows the entrant's actual AWS Builder ID.
